1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:01,596
A burnt chapati.

2
00:00:01,596 --> 00:00:02,658
A cup of tea.

3
00:00:02,658 --> 00:00:03,860
A pinch of salt.

4
00:00:04,260 --> 00:00:06,635
Diya forgot a chapati on the hot pan.

5
00:00:06,635 --> 00:00:09,958
When she came back, only a
little grey ash was left.

6
00:00:10,408 --> 00:00:14,887
She wants to be a doctor, and she
asked: the chapati was big, so where

7
00:00:14,887 --> 00:00:17,670
did most of it go, and what is this ash?

8
00:00:18,020 --> 00:00:22,429
Scientists use the same trick, and one
more, to find out what living things

9
00:00:22,429 --> 00:00:23,367
are made of.

10
00:00:23,867 --> 00:00:28,256
By the end, you will grind, strain
and burn a piece of tissue, and know

11
00:00:28,256 --> 00:00:29,857
what each step tells us.

12
00:00:30,507 --> 00:00:32,286
First, what are we looking for?

13
00:00:32,636 --> 00:00:36,799
Everything is made of elements,
like carbon, hydrogen and oxygen.

14
00:00:36,799 --> 00:00:41,184
An element is a pure substance that
cannot be split into simpler ones.

15
00:00:41,634 --> 00:00:45,291
Elements join to make compounds,
like water, sugar or salt.

16
00:00:45,741 --> 00:00:49,711
Living things and the rocks of the earth's
crust are made of the same elements.

17
00:00:49,711 --> 00:00:51,566
But the amounts are very different.

18
00:00:52,016 --> 00:00:56,149
Carbon is about 18.5 percent of the
human body by weight, but only 0.03

19
00:00:56,149 --> 00:00:57,964
percent of the earth's crust.

20
00:00:57,964 --> 00:01:02,041
Silicon is 27.7 percent of the
crust, and almost none of the body.

21
00:01:02,691 --> 00:01:06,358
Let's zoom into a living
tissue, one level at a time.

22
00:01:06,708 --> 00:01:09,191
Take a vegetable, or a piece of liver.

23
00:01:09,191 --> 00:01:10,547
It is living tissue.

24
00:01:11,047 --> 00:01:11,947
Zoom in.

25
00:01:11,766 --> 00:01:14,940
The tissue is made of
cells, packed side by side.

26
00:01:15,440 --> 00:01:16,635
Zoom into one cell.

27
00:01:16,635 --> 00:01:19,448
It is full of water, and
many dissolved chemicals.

28
00:01:19,948 --> 00:01:20,848
Zoom in further.

29
00:01:20,834 --> 00:01:24,593
We find small molecules, like sugars,
amino acids, fatty acids and nucleotides.

30
00:01:24,593 --> 00:01:26,379
These are what we want to pull out.

31
00:01:27,029 --> 00:01:31,482
Now watch method one, finding the
compounds, one step at a time.

32
00:01:31,832 --> 00:01:32,732
Step one.

33
00:01:32,537 --> 00:01:37,095
Take the living tissue, and put it in a
mortar, with an acid called trichloroacetic

34
00:01:37,095 --> 00:01:37,995
acid.

35
00:01:38,326 --> 00:01:39,226
Step two.

36
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:43,554
Grind it with a pestle, until it
becomes a thick slurry, like a paste.

37
00:01:44,304 --> 00:01:45,204
Step three.

38
00:01:45,146 --> 00:01:48,242
Strain the slurry through
a cheesecloth, or cotton.

39
00:01:48,992 --> 00:01:49,892
Step four.

40
00:01:49,846 --> 00:01:51,101
Two parts appear.

41
00:01:51,101 --> 00:01:55,328
The liquid that passes through is
the filtrate, called the acid-soluble

42
00:01:55,328 --> 00:01:56,228
pool.

43
00:01:55,877 --> 00:02:00,251
What stays on the cloth is the
retentate, the acid-insoluble fraction.

44
00:02:01,001 --> 00:02:01,901
Step five.

45
00:02:01,884 --> 00:02:05,569
The acid-soluble pool holds
thousands of organic compounds.

46
00:02:05,569 --> 00:02:10,775
Scientists separate each one, and work
out its formula and its likely structure.

47
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:13,469
Now method two, finding the elements.

48
00:02:13,819 --> 00:02:16,020
Weigh a small piece of living tissue.

49
00:02:16,020 --> 00:02:17,505
This is its wet weight.

50
00:02:18,005 --> 00:02:18,905
Dry it.

51
00:02:18,577 --> 00:02:19,961
All the water evaporates.

52
00:02:19,961 --> 00:02:21,797
What remains gives the dry weight.

53
00:02:22,297 --> 00:02:23,774
Now burn it fully.

54
00:02:23,774 --> 00:02:28,470
All the carbon compounds turn into
gases, like carbon dioxide and water

55
00:02:28,470 --> 00:02:30,648
vapour, and they float away.

56
00:02:31,148 --> 00:02:32,958
What remains is called ash.

57
00:02:32,958 --> 00:02:36,416
It holds inorganic elements,
like calcium and magnesium.

58
00:02:36,416 --> 00:02:38,731
That is the grey ash on Diya's pan.

59
00:02:39,231 --> 00:02:44,638
Inorganic compounds, like sulphate and
phosphate, also show up in the acid-soluble

60
00:02:44,638 --> 00:02:45,538
fraction.

61
00:02:46,107 --> 00:02:47,238
Side by side.

62
00:02:47,238 --> 00:02:48,138
How.

63
00:02:47,772 --> 00:02:51,468
Elemental analysis weighs,
dries and burns the tissue.

64
00:02:51,468 --> 00:02:54,966
Compound analysis grinds
it in acid and strains it.

65
00:02:55,716 --> 00:02:56,894
What it tells.

66
00:02:56,894 --> 00:03:01,471
Elemental analysis gives the elements,
like hydrogen, oxygen, chlorine

67
00:03:01,471 --> 00:03:02,430
and carbon.

68
00:03:02,430 --> 00:03:06,912
Compound analysis gives the kinds
of organic and inorganic compounds.

69
00:03:07,662 --> 00:03:08,562
Examples.

70
00:03:08,311 --> 00:03:10,368
The ash shows calcium and magnesium.

71
00:03:10,368 --> 00:03:12,732
The filtrate shows amino acids and sugars.

72
00:03:13,482 --> 00:03:14,382
Your turn.

73
00:03:14,337 --> 00:03:16,558
You burn a dried leaf completely.

74
00:03:16,558 --> 00:03:20,843
What is left in the ash: carbon
compounds, magnesium, or water?

75
00:03:20,843 --> 00:03:22,101
Pause and decide.

76
00:03:25,301 --> 00:03:26,201
Magnesium!

77
00:03:26,099 --> 00:03:29,843
Water left on drying, and
carbon compounds left as gases.

78
00:03:29,843 --> 00:03:32,167
Inorganic elements stay in the ash.

79
00:03:32,817 --> 00:03:33,717
Remember this.

80
00:03:33,707 --> 00:03:35,238
Burning shows the elements.

81
00:03:35,238 --> 00:03:36,853
Grinding shows the compounds.

82
00:03:37,453 --> 00:03:39,053
Now, a worked example.

83
00:03:39,053 --> 00:03:40,568
Six things in a leaf.

84
00:03:40,568 --> 00:03:44,224
After drying and burning,
are they in the ash, or gone?

85
00:03:44,574 --> 00:03:45,474
Calcium.

86
00:03:45,302 --> 00:03:46,821
An inorganic element.

87
00:03:46,821 --> 00:03:47,826
In the ash.

88
00:03:48,276 --> 00:03:49,601
Glucose, a sugar.

89
00:03:49,601 --> 00:03:50,877
A carbon compound.

90
00:03:50,877 --> 00:03:52,010
Gone, as gas.

91
00:03:52,460 --> 00:03:53,360
Magnesium.

92
00:03:53,187 --> 00:03:54,087
In the ash.

93
00:03:54,516 --> 00:03:55,416
Water.

94
00:03:54,899 --> 00:03:56,250
Gone, it evaporated on drying.

95
00:03:56,700 --> 00:03:57,633
Amino acids.

96
00:03:57,633 --> 00:03:58,806
Carbon compounds.

97
00:03:58,806 --> 00:03:59,941
Gone, as gas.

98
00:04:00,391 --> 00:04:01,294
And sodium.

99
00:04:01,294 --> 00:04:02,747
An inorganic element.

100
00:04:02,747 --> 00:04:03,709
In the ash.

101
00:04:04,359 --> 00:04:05,820
Now, the exam trap.

102
00:04:06,170 --> 00:04:08,184
Many students mix up the two parts.

103
00:04:08,184 --> 00:04:10,831
They think the retentate
is the acid-soluble pool.

104
00:04:11,231 --> 00:04:13,973
But the filtrate passes through the cloth.

105
00:04:13,973 --> 00:04:15,953
That is the acid-soluble pool.

106
00:04:15,953 --> 00:04:17,775
The retentate stays behind.

107
00:04:17,775 --> 00:04:20,052
That is the acid-insoluble fraction.

108
00:04:20,702 --> 00:04:22,016
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:22,016 --> 00:04:26,239
In board papers, as the steps of the
analysis, and in NEET, as statement

110
00:04:26,239 --> 00:04:29,272
questions on the two fractions
and the element table.

111
00:04:29,872 --> 00:04:31,019
Exam level now.

112
00:04:31,019 --> 00:04:32,807
Which statement is correct?

113
00:04:32,807 --> 00:04:34,002
Pause and solve.

114
00:04:39,402 --> 00:04:40,738
The answer is C.

115
00:04:41,238 --> 00:04:44,673
Carbon is far more common in
the body than in the crust.

116
00:04:44,673 --> 00:04:47,470
And the retentate is the
acid-insoluble fraction.

117
00:04:47,970 --> 00:04:50,732
Silicon is almost absent from the body.

118
00:04:50,732 --> 00:04:52,197
Only C is correct.

119
00:04:52,797 --> 00:04:54,337
Here is your recap.

120
00:04:54,337 --> 00:04:55,920
Screenshot this card.

121
00:04:59,120 --> 00:05:01,306
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

122
00:05:01,706 --> 00:05:02,606
One.

123
00:05:02,225 --> 00:05:04,263
What is the acid-soluble pool?

124
00:05:05,163 --> 00:05:06,063
Two.

125
00:05:05,575 --> 00:05:08,563
What is the difference between
wet weight and dry weight?

126
00:05:09,463 --> 00:05:10,363
Three.

127
00:05:09,950 --> 00:05:12,378
Why are there no carbon
compounds in the ash?

128
00:05:13,478 --> 00:05:18,243
Next time: why does a plant make rubber,
or a scent, that it does not seem

129
00:05:18,243 --> 00:05:19,143
to need?

130
00:05:18,975 --> 00:05:21,999
We find out in primary
and secondary metabolites.
